    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nWith `CYTHON_USE_MODULE_STATE`, importing a module again after removing it from `sys.modules` crashes the process.\n\nWhat happens:\n\n1. On the second import, `__pyx_pymod_create` gives back the module that already exists ([ModuleSetupCode.c#L1781](https://github.com/cython/cython/blob/997aeea7410c0b7e0904efab62553145064f2ff8/Cython/Utility/ModuleSetupCode.c#L1781-L1782)).\n2. CPython drops the state of whatever `Py_mod_create` returns and gives it a new, empty one ([moduleobject.c#L361](https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/58ed60b7415e218ce3d608302e39b5e55bfb0e88/Objects/moduleobject.c#L361)).\n3. The exec function sees the module as already set up and returns early ([ModuleNode.py#L3449](https://github.com/cython/cython/blob/997aeea7410c0b7e0904efab62553145064f2ff8/Cython/Compiler/ModuleNode.py#L3449-L3454)), so the new state is never filled.\n\nAfter that, every module state lookup returns NULL. The `Py_mod_create` docs say it should return a new module object ([module.rst#L311-L314](https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/4489dc3e16abb09aa2e50a4909ed33f2327ae77d/Doc/c-api/module.rst#L311-L314)).\n\nNo crash without module state, or with `CYTHON_PEP489_MULTI_PHASE_INIT=0`.\n\nFound through asyncpg 0.31+ (MagicStack/asyncpg#1292), where coverage.py and Temporal's sandbox re-import it.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n```cython\n# demo.pyx\ncdef class Base:\n    pass\n\ncdef class Child(Base):\n    def __init__(self):\n        Base.__init__(self)\n\ndef make():\n    return Child()\n```\n\n```python\n# setup.py\nfrom Cython.Build import cythonize\nfrom setuptools import Extension, setup\n\nsetup(\n    ext_modules=cythonize(\n        [Extension(\"demo\", [\"demo.pyx\"], define_macros=[(\"CYTHON_USE_MODULE_STATE\", \"1\")])],\n        language_level=3,\n    )\n)\n```\n\n```python\n# run.py\nimport sys\n\nimport demo\n\nprint(type(demo.make()).__name__, flush=True)\ndel sys.modules[\"demo\"]\nimport demo\n\nprint(type(demo.make()).__name__, flush=True)\n```\n\n```\n$ python setup.py build_ext --inplace && python -X faulthandler run.py\nChild\nFatal Python error: Segmentation fault\n\nCurrent thread 0x00000001f856dd80 (most recent call first):\n  File \".../run.py\", line 9 in <module>\n```\n\nWithout the define it prints `Child` twice.\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nThe second import raises, and the first one keeps working. This change does that for me:\n\n```diff\n-    if (${module_cname})\n+    if (${module_cname}) {\n+    #if CYTHON_USE_MODULE_STATE\n+        // CPython resets md_state of the module that Py_mod_create returns,\n+        // so handing back the live module would wipe its state.\n+        PyErr_Format(PyExc_ImportError,\n+            \"Module '%s' has already been imported into this interpreter. Re-importing it is not supported.\",\n+            def->m_name);\n+        return NULL;\n+    #else\n         return __Pyx_NewRef(${module_cname});\n+    #endif\n+    }\n```\n\nWith it, the repro raises `ImportError` and the first `demo.make()` still works. asyncpg built against it raises the same `ImportError`, and its subinterpreter tests still pass. Happy to open a PR with a `tests/run` test if this is the right direction.\n\n### OS\n\nmacOS 26, arm64\n\n### Python version\n\n3.12.13 (also 3.13.13, 3.14.6)\n\n### Cython version\n\n3.3.0 and master 997aeea74\n",
